C EXAMPLE - HELLO: WRITE, COMPILE, ASSEMBLE, RUN

PURPOSE: Write one line without printf or any C library.
Preflight: get_manual(topic='languages/c'); help cc; help as.
Create /tmp/hello.c using MCP write_file (path=/tmp/hello.c,
content is the following plain ASCII source, WITHOUT fences):

  int main() {
      write(1, "Hello\n", 6);
      return 0;
  }

Then run these as THREE separate execute_command calls:
  cc /tmp/hello.c
  as /tmp/hello.s
  /tmp/a.out
Expected stdout: Hello followed by a newline.

WHY: cc translates to Thumb-1 assembly; as creates /tmp/a.out.
The C compiler does NOT create an executable by itself.
A new 'as' run overwrites /tmp/a.out: check its success before run.
On error: inspect /tmp/hello.s with cat; see /man/c-debug.
Constraints: source <=1024 B; no #include or printf.
MCP: do not combine all commands until individual outcomes are known.
